1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Astronomers carved it into 88
pieces.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Today’s 88 began with Ptolemy’s 48,
listed nearly 1,900 years ago.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
But Ptolemy never saw the far southern
sky.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
In the late 1500s, Dutch navigators
charted those stars.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Plancius made 12 new constellations: a
peacock, a toucan, a chameleon, a flying
fish…

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Hevelius’s 1690 atlas filled northern
and equatorial gaps. For the Lynx, he
said you need a lynx’s eyes.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
In the 1750s, Lacaille measured nearly
10,000 stars at the Cape of Good Hope.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
His 14 new constellations are almost all
instruments: telescope, microscope,
clock, air pump…

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
And one mountain: Cape Town’s Table
Mountain — Mensa.

10
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
In 1922, the new International
Astronomical Union set the list: 88,
each with a three-letter code like UMa.

11
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
A list has no borders. Belgian
astronomer Delporte drew them:

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
straight lines along the sky’s grid.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Approved in 1928, published in 1930: the
whole sky, no gaps.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Now a constellation is a plot of sky:
every point belongs to exactly one.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The largest, Hydra, is 19 times the size
of the Southern Cross.

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Matariki: when the stars rise, the new
year begins.

17
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

